      "awards": [
        "GeoHipster Calendar 2026 - Featured",
        "Atlas of Sustainability - Featured"
      ],
      "coverImage": "/images/projects/earth-on-fire/dark.webp",
      "context": "Developed during the week design challenge in TU Wien with a guest lecture from Wisconsin University - Robert Roth. The topic was about Sustainable Development Goals (SDG)",
      "idea": "For this map, we had to choose the topic from 17 SDGs and then make a map within 3 days in a group of three people. Climate Action - 13 SDG was general topic. For the map idea, I came up with fire point datasets since I worked with it for another project abut analyzing ignition points in Kazakhstan.",
      "design": "I was responsible for data collection and processing as well as overall layout and map design. I wanted to create a shining effect since the topic is fire points, so it visually easy to see where the earth burns more often. Map uses persuasive cartographic and text techniques to emphasize the problem with wildfires, agricultural burnings, deforestation, which leads to a higher CO2 level.",
      "inspiration": "Inspiration was driven from visualization of California 100 years of wildfire.",
      "process": [
        {
          "step": 1,
          "title": "FIRMS Data Processing",
          "description": "Downloaded data from FIRMS website for the global view for five years. To clean the data I used python since too many points in QGIS are laggy."
        },
        {
          "step": 2,
          "title": "Styling & Export",
          "description": "The task was to visualize millions of points on the map without creating visual noise and keep it visually understandable and pleasant. ",
          "image": "/images/projects/earth-on-fire/close-details.webp"
        }
      ],
      "outcome": "Featured in the GeoHipster 2026 Calendar and in the Atlas of Sustainability book.",
      "stack": [
